Kosh Global IT — Global Display Rules, Advanced Data Grid and Multi-Company/Multi-Branch Functionality
1. Objective
Enhance the existing My Kosh Innovations Global IT System by implementing a consistent, reusable display and data-grid standard across all applicable modules.
The system is a multi-company, multi-branch enterprise platform. All screens must follow the correct company, branch, organization, and data-ownership rules.
Preserve the existing UI design, navigation, sidebar, colors, typography, cards, forms, and overall visual appearance. Do not create a new application or redesign the system.
Implement the required changes directly in the existing project. Inspect the current code, identify gaps, and correct the implementation without breaking existing functionality.
2. Remove Redundant Context Displays
Remove redundant company, branch, environment, and SAP database context panels from module screens where the same information is already available elsewhere and is not needed for the current operation.
Specifically:
•	Remove unnecessary repeated Active Company and Active Branch display panels.
•	Remove duplicate company/branch context banners.
•	Remove redundant environment and SAP database information banners from ordinary master-data screens.
•	Remove unnecessary selected-branch summary panels that consume space without providing essential functionality.
•	Remove other duplicated contextual information identified during the code audit.
Keep the existing user identity, role, notification controls, navigation, and other useful header functionality.
Do not remove the underlying company registry, branch registry, environment configuration, SAP integration settings, authorization rules, or data relationships.
Do not remove essential company or branch identification from records, forms, reports, or sensitive operations. Display required information within the relevant screen or operation in a compact, meaningful way.
The objective is to simplify the display without weakening the multi-company, multi-branch architecture.
3. Company and Branch Identification
Add the appropriate organizational identification fields to all applicable data grids.
For company-dependent records, provide:
•	Company Code
•	Company Name
For branch-dependent records, provide:
•	Branch Code
•	Branch Name
Where a record is associated with both a company and a branch, display all four fields.
Retrieve these values from the existing authoritative company and branch registries. Do not hard-code values or create duplicate company records.
For the Warehouse Setup & Multi-Warehouse Register, use this default column order:
1.	Company Code
2.	Company Name
3.	Branch Code
4.	Branch Name
5.	Warehouse Code
6.	Warehouse Name
7.	Default Policy
8.	Nettable
9.	Bin Locations
10.	Status
11.	Actions
Preserve additional existing business-critical columns where required.
Apply the same identification standard to Global Item Master, Create Item, Custom Specification, Item Code and Description, Branch Item Availability, BP Master, Warehouse Master, and other relevant modules.
For genuinely global records, show an appropriate Global or Organization-wide scope indicator. Do not incorrectly associate global records with an arbitrary company or branch.
4. Reusable Enterprise Data Grid
Create or enhance a reusable data-grid component that can be configured for each module.
The grid must support the following capabilities.
4.1 Search
Provide a search field that searches across the configured searchable columns.
Examples include:
•	Company Code and Company Name
•	Branch Code and Branch Name
•	Item Code and Item Description
•	BP Code and BP Name
•	Warehouse Code and Warehouse Name
•	Status and other applicable fields
Search must operate on the actual dataset, including records beyond the current page when server-side pagination is used.
Provide clear-search functionality and a useful no-results message.
4.2 Standard Filters
Provide filters for applicable fields, including:
•	Company
•	Branch
•	Item or BP code
•	Description or name
•	Warehouse
•	Status
•	Other module-specific fields
Filters must be configurable according to the data model of each module.
Company and branch dropdowns must display meaningful codes and names and must include only companies and branches authorized for the current user.
Allow users to combine multiple filter conditions.
4.3 Advanced Filters
Provide an Advanced Filters feature supporting:
•	Field selection
•	Operator selection
•	Filter values
•	Multiple conditions
•	AND/OR grouping where supported
•	Add and remove condition controls
•	Apply filters
•	Clear all filters
Support appropriate operators according to field type, such as:
•	Equals
•	Not equals
•	Contains
•	Starts with
•	Ends with
•	Greater than
•	Less than
•	Is empty
•	Is not empty
•	Date range
Do not offer operators that are incompatible with the selected field.
Validate filter definitions on the backend and prevent unsafe query construction.
4.4 Configurable Columns
Provide a Configure Columns option that allows authorized users to:
•	Show or hide available columns
•	Reorder columns
•	Change the displayed column sequence
•	Restore default column settings
•	Save personal column preferences where supported
Each module must define its permitted columns and default layout.
Preserve mandatory identification and governance fields where hiding them could make a record or operation ambiguous.
Column configuration must not require database schema changes merely to control visibility or ordering.
4.5 Single-Select and Multi-Select
Support both selection modes.
Single-select:
•	Select one record.
•	Clearly highlight the selected row.
•	Pass the correct record ID to the applicable action.
Multi-select:
•	Select multiple records using checkboxes.
•	Support selecting all records on the current page.
•	Where supported, allow selecting all filtered records with a clear confirmation and accurate record count.
•	Provide Clear Selection.
•	Display the number of selected records.
Maintain correct selection when the user sorts, filters, or changes pages.
Selection must be based on stable record IDs, not row positions.
Do not introduce new high-impact bulk actions, such as bulk deletion or bulk synchronization, without approval.
4.6 Printing
Provide a Print action.
The print output must:
•	Include the module title.
•	Include relevant Company Code and Company Name.
•	Include relevant Branch Code and Branch Name.
•	Include the applied filters.
•	Include the configured columns and applicable records.
•	Use a clean, readable layout.
•	Exclude navigation menus and unrelated screen controls.
Support printing selected records or filtered results where appropriate.
Clearly identify whether the operation prints selected records, the current page, or the entire filtered result set. Do not silently omit records that the user expects to print.
4.7 Export
Provide an Export action supporting:
•	CSV
•	XLSX, using the existing stack or an appropriate established library
Exports must respect:
•	User authorization
•	Company and branch scope
•	Applied filters
•	Selected columns
•	Selected-record scope, where applicable
•	Pagination-independent export of all filtered records where requested
Include the relevant company and branch identifiers and a clear indication of the exported scope.
Do not export unauthorized records, credentials, session tokens, private keys, or other secrets.
5. Global Display Configuration
Implement reusable configuration for the grid standard.
Each module configuration should define:
•	Module identifier
•	Available columns
•	Default visible columns
•	Default column order
•	Searchable fields
•	Filterable fields
•	Advanced-filter operators
•	Sortable fields
•	Single-select and multi-select support
•	Print support
•	Supported export formats
•	Mandatory identification fields
•	Module-specific restrictions
Reuse the application's existing configuration and persistence mechanisms wherever possible.
Do not create an unnecessarily complex configuration framework if the existing architecture can support the requirement.
6. Multi-Company and Multi-Branch Behavior
The entire application must continue to operate as a multi-company, multi-branch system.
For administrators authorized to access multiple companies, do not require a single global Active Company or Active Branch selection before they can use the system.
Use screen-level company and branch filters to define the scope of the current view or operation.
Requirements:
•	Support multiple-company filtering where appropriate.
•	Provide All Authorized Companies and All Authorized Branches options where the module supports them.
•	Restrict branch options to valid branches within the selected company scope.
•	Refresh the data when filters change.
•	Prevent stale data from a previous company or branch from remaining visible.
•	Require explicit company or branch selection in creation forms only when the business rules require it.
•	Do not automatically assign a record to the first available company or branch.
The backend must validate every requested company and branch against the authenticated user's permissions. A filter is not an authorization mechanism.
Preserve the correct distinction between global, organization-wide, company-specific, branch-specific, and environment-specific records.
7. Apply the Standard to Existing Modules
Implement and validate the reusable standard in the following modules:
1.	Global Item Master
2.	Create Item
3.	Custom Specification
4.	Item Code and Description
5.	Branch Item Availability Matrix
6.	BP Master
7.	Warehouse Master
8.	Organization & Branch
9.	Warehouse Setup & Defaults
10.	Field & Rule Configurations
11.	SAP Integration Management
12.	Company Details Field Mapping
13.	Fetch Company Details & Sync
14.	Creation & Scheduling Profiles
15.	Integration Testing
16.	Approval Workflows
17.	Other existing modules that maintain company-dependent or branch-dependent records.
Do not add irrelevant filters to genuinely global modules. Determine the correct data scope before implementing each grid.
8. SAP Integration Safeguards
Keep the existing SAP Business One HANA company registry, environment configuration, Service Layer connections, and synchronization rules.
The removal of redundant context displays must not remove SAP integration safeguards.
For SAP operations:
•	Explicitly identify the Kosh company.
•	Use the configured SAP company database and environment.
•	Validate synchronization activation and authorization.
•	Apply the correct source-to-target mapping.
•	Record the company, environment, job ID, and execution result.
•	Preserve correct global and company-specific master-data relationships.
Do not infer the SAP company database from a global header selection or branch selection.
Do not introduce unsupported direct writes to SAP system tables or bypass TLS certificate validation.
9. Performance, Security, and Data Integrity
Inspect the existing backend, APIs, database schema, and authorization model before implementation.
For large datasets:
•	Use server-side filtering, sorting, pagination, and export where necessary.
•	Validate requested filter fields and operators.
•	Avoid loading unlimited datasets into the browser.
•	Prevent unauthorized cross-company and cross-branch access.
•	Validate company/branch relationships on all relevant read and write operations.
•	Preserve stable record IDs and existing master-data relationships.
•	Avoid duplicate queries and unnecessary network requests.
Do not perform destructive production changes or reassign existing records without an approved migration plan.
10. Acceptance Tests
Execute and report actual test results for:
1.	Redundant context displays are removed without breaking required functions.
2.	Required company and branch fields are visible in applicable grids.
3.	Search returns correct records.
4.	Standard filters work independently and together.
5.	Advanced filters support valid fields and operators.
6.	Column visibility and reordering work.
7.	Default column settings can be restored.
8.	Single-select works correctly.
9.	Multi-select and selection counts work correctly.
10.	Selection remains correct after sorting and pagination.
11.	Printing respects the requested record scope.
12.	CSV and XLSX exports contain the correct filtered records.
13.	Export respects the selected columns and authorization scope.
14.	Company-specific records do not leak across companies.
15.	Branch-specific records are associated with the correct company.
16.	Global records retain their intended scope.
17.	Existing create, edit, and other module functions continue to work.
18.	SAP synchronization continues to use its explicit company/environment configuration.
Use mock services or test data where appropriate. Do not execute destructive tests against production.
11. Implementation and Completion Report
Implement the changes in the existing project.
Start with the Warehouse Setup & Multi-Warehouse Register as the reference implementation. Validate it, then reuse the same grid component and configuration pattern in the other applicable modules.
Provide a completion report identifying:
•	Files and components changed
•	Screens updated
•	Context displays removed
•	Columns added
•	Search and filter functionality implemented
•	Column configuration functionality implemented
•	Selection behavior implemented
•	Print and export functionality implemented
•	Backend authorization and filtering changes
•	Database migrations, if any
•	Automated test results
•	Screenshots or preview evidence
•	Remaining issues and pending work
Do not claim success based only on the visual appearance of the screen. Verify the actual functionality and data behavior.
Final requirement: implement the Global Display Rule as a reusable enterprise standard across Kosh Global IT, preserving the current design and ensuring that all relevant screens operate correctly within the multi-company, multi-branch architecture.



