Import workflow
1.	Select the SAP company.
2.	Select the environment: PROD, UAT, or DEV.
3.	Select the applicable module or choose all modules represented by the template.
4.	Download the current Excel mapping template.
5.	Upload the completed Excel file.
6.	Validate the workbook and display a preview.
7.	Show new mappings, existing mappings, proposed updates, duplicate rows, invalid values, unsupported fields, and missing mandatory fields.
8.	Allow the user to choose whether valid new mappings should be created and whether existing mappings may be updated.
9.	Require explicit confirmation before applying changes.
10.	Display the final import summary and provide a downloadable error report when applicable.
Template requirements
The Excel template must contain, at minimum, appropriate columns for:
•	Template version
•	Kosh company code
•	Kosh company name
•	SAP company database ID
•	Environment
•	Module/entity
•	SAP source object
•	SAP source field/property
•	SAP display label
•	Kosh target entity
•	Kosh target field/property
•	Data type
•	Mapping/transformation rule
•	Mandatory indicator
•	Synchronization direction
•	Read/write capability
•	Module sync enabled status
•	Mapping status
•	Remarks
Add further columns only where required by the existing data model or verified SAP API metadata.
Provide a Download Template action and an Export Existing Mappings action.
The template must be versioned. Validate the company code, SAP database ID, environment, module, source property, target property, data type, and other applicable fields before importing.
Import safety
•	Never silently overwrite existing mappings.
•	Detect duplicates using an appropriate mapping key.
•	Show the before-and-after values for proposed updates.
•	Reject or flag invalid and unsupported mappings.
•	Support partial imports only when valid rows can be safely processed independently.
•	Provide a dry-run preview before committing changes.
•	Record the file name, template version, user, company, environment, timestamp, result, and affected mapping count in the audit history.
•	Do not include credentials or secrets in the Excel file.
•	Do not automatically activate synchronization merely because a mapping was imported.
5. Company and environment isolation
This is a multi-company, multi-environment SAP integration system.
All configuration and data operations must use the correct company and environment identifiers.
The company code and SAP company database ID must be visible wherever the user configures, reviews, imports, exports, or executes company-specific mappings.
Never identify a company using its display name alone when a stable company ID is available.
All connection tests, field discovery, mapping operations, sync jobs, and import/export operations must be isolated by company and environment.
A template intended for one company/environment must not be silently applied to another. Require an explicit reassignment and revalidation if cross-company reuse is supported.
