Objective
Enhance the existing Global Item Master and related item-management modules in My Kosh Innovations Global IT System.
Kosh is a multi-company, multi-branch system. A single global item can have different operational categories and usage classifications at different branches.
Example:
•	Pondicherry branch: Item-1001 is classified as a Finished Good (FG).
•	Chennai branch: The same Item-1001 is classified as a Raw Material (RM).
These are two branch-specific classifications of one global item, not two different global items.
Implement this using the existing project architecture, database, UI components, authorization model, and master-data workflows. Do not create a new standalone application.
1. Global Item Master
Maintain one authoritative global item identity containing:
•	Global Item ID.
•	Global Item Code.
•	Base Item Description.
•	Base Unit of Measure.
•	Global lifecycle status.
•	Global default category, if applicable.
•	Other genuinely shared item attributes.
The Global Item ID must remain stable when branch classifications change.
Prevent duplicate global items using configured uniqueness and duplicate-detection rules. Do not use the item description alone as the unique identifier.
2. Branch Item Classification
Create or enhance a branch-specific item classification record linked to:
•	Global Item ID.
•	Company ID.
•	Branch ID.
•	Branch-specific category.
•	Branch-specific item role.
•	Local usage and inventory settings, as applicable.
•	Branch-specific status.
•	Effective-from and effective-to dates, where required.
•	Approval status and audit metadata.
Support configurable categories such as:
•	Raw Material (RM).
•	Semi-Finished Good (SFG).
•	Finished Good (FG).
•	Trading Item.
•	Consumable.
•	Packaging Material.
•	Service or Non-Inventory Item.
Categories must be configurable through the existing category or classification configuration rather than hardcoded into the UI.
Allow the same Global Item ID to have different categories in different branches and, where authorized, different companies.
3. Company and Branch Relationships
Enforce the following relationships:
•	A global item can be referenced by multiple companies and branches.
•	Every branch classification must reference a valid Global Item ID.
•	Every branch classification must belong to the correct company and branch.
•	Company and branch authorization must be validated on the backend.
•	Company and branch codes must be visible in relevant item lists and detail views.
•	Branch filters must show only classifications belonging to the selected company and branch.
Do not assume that branch codes, item codes, or company-specific SAP item codes are globally unique.
4. Item Management Screens
Global Item Master
Show the shared global identity and shared attributes. Provide a way to view all company and branch classifications associated with the selected item.
Create Item
Create the global item once. Provide a separate workflow for assigning the item to one or more companies and branches, with appropriate permissions and validations.
Do not automatically activate an item in every branch simply because the global item was created.
Custom Specifications
Keep shared specifications at the global level where appropriate. Support branch-specific specifications only when the business rules explicitly permit them. Define how conflicting specification values are handled.
Item Code & Description
Maintain separate mappings for the Global Item Code, company-specific SAP Item Code, and optional branch-specific display description. Do not overwrite a global description merely because a branch uses a different description.
Branch Item Availability
Display the selected company's branch-specific item classification and availability. Distinguish:
•	Item assigned to branch.
•	Item active or inactive at branch.
•	Item permitted for purchasing.
•	Item permitted for production.
•	Item permitted for sales.
•	Item permitted for inventory, where applicable.
•	Actual stock availability, if inventory quantities are integrated.
Do not infer actual stock quantity from a branch availability flag.
5. SAP Business One HANA Integration
Maintain a mapping between the Kosh Global Item ID and the SAP item identifier for each SAP company database.
Do not assume that the same SAP ItemCode exists in every SAP company.
Configure item classification synchronization by company, branch and module according to supported SAP fields and Kosh ownership rules.
Important:
•	A Kosh branch-specific category must not automatically overwrite a shared SAP item category or group.
•	If SAP Business One does not support the desired branch-level category as a native field, retain it in Kosh or use an explicitly configured SAP UDF or supported extension.
•	Discover the actual SAP Service Layer properties and supported fields before implementing mappings.
•	Do not invent SAP fields or claim unsupported branch-specific functionality.
•	Preview mapping differences and validate data before importing or exporting.
•	Respect independent environment, company and module activation controls.
6. Validation and Audit
•	Prevent duplicate global items.
•	Prevent orphaned branch classifications.
•	Validate company and branch ownership.
•	Record changes to branch category, item role, status, and effective dates.
•	Record old value, new value, user, timestamp, company, branch, and change source.
•	Apply configured approvals before sensitive classification changes take effect.
•	Do not silently change another branch's classification when one branch is updated.
7. Search, Filters and Export
Provide search and advanced filters for:
•	Global Item Code.
•	Global Item Description.
•	Company Code and Name.
•	Branch Code and Name.
•	Branch-specific Category.
•	Item Role.
•	Branch Status.
•	SAP Company Database and SAP Item Code.
Support configurable columns, single-select and multi-select actions where authorized, printing, and export.
Exports must include company and branch context so that different classifications of the same global item are distinguishable.
8. Acceptance Tests
1.	Create one global item.
2.	Assign it to the Pondicherry branch and classify it as FG.
3.	Assign the same Global Item ID to the Chennai branch and classify it as RM.
4.	Confirm that only one global item exists.
5.	Confirm that both branches retain their own classifications.
6.	Change the Pondicherry classification and verify that Chennai is unchanged.
7.	Verify company and branch filters, user permissions, and exports.
8.	Verify that each SAP company uses its correct item mapping.
9.	Verify that failed synchronization and retry operations do not create duplicate items.
10.	Verify that global and branch-level changes appear correctly in the audit history.
Inspect the existing source code and data model before implementing. Reuse existing components and records, preserve working functionality, run available tests and builds, and report changed files and results.
Do not introduce mock data, duplicate global items, or destructive database changes.

